---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import StoryChapter from "../components/StoryChapter.astro";
import WorkList from "../components/WorkList.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import GroundingLoop from "../components/GroundingLoop.jsx";
---

<Layout
  story
  title="Curtis Murray · AI Engineer — grounding LLMs in something you can trust"
>
  <Hero />

  <StoryChapter
    id="thesis"
    num="01"
    eyebrow="The fix"
    title="Your LLM is confidently wrong, and you can't see where."
  >
    <p>
      Constrain it to reason over real, verified entities and it can't invent
      what isn't there. Evaluate every tool call and the reasoning has nowhere
      to hide.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is how I built the first working morphological analyser for spoken
      Tamil — literary Tamil has had analysers for years; spoken Tamil had none.
    </p>
  </StoryChapter>

  <!-- Interactive centerpiece: the propose → ground → dispose loop -->
  <section id="grounding" class="relative py-12 sm:py-20">
    <div class="container mx-auto px-6 lg:px-16">
      <div class="io-reveal mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <p
          class="text-sm font-medium tracking-[0.16em] uppercase text-primary mb-2 text-center"
        >
          The pattern, made tangible
        </p>
        <h2
          class="font-slab font-bold text-base-content text-3xl sm:text-[2.5rem] tracking-tight text-center mb-8"
        >
          Watch it refuse to make things up.
        </h2>
        <GroundingLoop client:visible />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <StoryChapter
    num="02"
    eyebrow="The throughline"
    title="I've been doing one thing my whole career."
  >
    <p>
      The tools keep changing. The job doesn't: <strong
        class="text-base-content">messy, unstructured data in — trustworthy
        structure out.</strong
      > My PhD was applied maths and NLP, pulling the hidden structure out of online
      health narratives with methods you could inspect and explain, not black boxes.
      The result I'm proudest of: we picked up COVID anosmia signals in Reddit data
      before they were widely recognised clinically.
    </p>
    <p>
      I took the same instinct into a clinical project at RMIT — mapping messy
      free-text notes onto verified medical entities — and beat string matching,
      ClinicalBERT, and raw LLM generation by grounding every answer in a
      trusted source instead of letting the model freelance.
    </p>
  </StoryChapter>

  <WorkList />

  <StoryChapter
    num="03"
    eyebrow="In production"
    title="And it has to actually work."
  >
    <p>
      I'm not only a researcher. I shipped an optimisation model at an energy
      company that closed a $600k licence sale, and I build full-stack — so I
      care about the thing running in production, not just in a notebook.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now that LLMs are this capable I lean on them heavily, and I make up for
      their opacity by moving the rigour into the system around them:
      traceability, tool-call evaluation, and a deterministic core the model
      can't override.
    </p>
  </StoryChapter>

  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>
